From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Elias Bachaalany <elias.bachaalany@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 18:00:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 05/11] Decompiler: edit a structure and rename a function in
 place

A client that keeps one Architecture for a whole session (an offline
program database, not a host that re-sends everything per decompile) has to
edit what it already holds. Two edits had no way to do that.

TypeFactory::setFields only accepts an incomplete structure, so the only way to
change a complete one was destroyType + a new type: every pointer, array,
typedef and prototype that referred to the old object was left pointing at
freed memory, and a type keyed by its id lost its identity. Add
TypeFactory::redefineStruct, which replaces the fields of an existing
structure in place (erasing and reinserting it in the type tree, whose order
depends on the fields), gives its typedef copies the same fields, and keeps
its name, id and every reference to it.

A Funcdata keeps its own copy of its name, which declarations and call sites
print, while renameSymbol renames only the FunctionSymbol. Add
Funcdata::setName so a rename reaches both; the caller renames the symbol.

Measured on the rich_fixture project (198 functions): a member rename on a
PDB structure reached the decompilation of the function that reads it
(s->wc_width), and the struct kept its id, its category path and its size.
